00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  across the country  help is wanted and businesses can't fill  positions fast enough  i'm in a war for talent if you do not  jump to make an offer to an applicant  at the end of the interview they are  lost and increasingly  it takes more than an offer wawa is  growing many jobs now come with  incentives like sign on bonuses  500 new hire bonus and freebies have  been offered for just showing up to an  interview  but with more than 9 million people  still out of work and a record number of  jobs available  why are these additional offers needed  and are they working  what we're seeing in this unusual labor  market is a resurgence  in leverage for lower wage workers and  we really haven't seen that  in this country for at least 50 years  before the pandemic the labor market was  tight with a jobless rate near a 50-year  low  then millions lost their jobs hourly  restaurant  retail and hotel workers were hit  particularly hard  now as quickly as the economy has  reopened those employers are trying to  staff back up  but many workers remain on the sideline  for a variety of reasons  including coveted concerns child care  and extended unemployment benefits  employers right now are frantically  trying to make sure that they can take  advantage  of the opening up of the economy and  salvage what has been an impossible last  14 15 months for them by ramping up  their businesses very quickly  but it's hard to do that if you don't  have all your workers  in place that's something omni hotels  and resorts knows well  in february i had 200 jobs open  today i have 2 800 jobs open  and i've hired 5000 since february  so we are having explosive  demand to get those roles filled the  company like many others  has turned to incentives including free  housing for summer workers in certain  markets a 250  signing bonus three free nights at an  omni hotel and a set of knives for new  culinary hires  workers who stay past labor day get  another 500  and entrance into omni's management  development program we're used to  interviewing people and having several  applicants for a role and  taking our time and having them come  back for a second interview  you can't do that anymore for it's hard  to fill culinary roles omni recently  decided  we'll add two trainers to help give  inexperienced applicants  on the job training i think a lot of  companies would say oh  you've never cooked you've never been in  a kitchen we don't need you and i'm like  look  five people that want to work for us  bring them in we'll find a way to make  it work  omni's incentives come on top of wage  increases  the company says hourly wages were up as  much as 20 from a year ago in some  markets  that bump isn't just to compete with  other hotels both target and walmart  have agreed to raise wages this year  and amazon is offering a thousand dollar  signing bonus in some locations  they're going after the same labor i'm  going after  and i think that was a big wake-up call  for the hotel industry  labor department data show average  weekly wages in leisure and hospitality  have increased 10.7 percent since the  start of the pandemic  outpacing the private sector overall and  that's not always the case right a lot  of times it's  higher tax higher educated workers that  see the bigger pay increases  right now we're seeing the reverse and  that's why you have all this activity  around bonuses  but are wage increases and job  incentives actually drawing workers back  into the workforce  it's a mixed bag omni has hired about 3  000 people since it began offering  incentives labor department data show  that the sector overall  added the most jobs in june suggesting  the incentives  may be helping we still have a lot of  job openings millions of job openings in  these  these sectors so it suggests that either  some employers are going to need to have  to do even more  or they're going to have to wait it out  and see if the labor market finds its  level in the fall  one reason the fall is important that's  when the federal government's expanded  unemployment benefits are said to expire  nationwide  though more than 20 states have already  stopped paying the extra 300  a week payments some economists point to  that benefit  as one reason why workers aren't rushing  to fill open jobs  many people on unemployment benefits are  earning you know the equivalent of  working full-time for 15 an hour so if  you're offering less than that or about  that it's pretty hard to attract workers  a recent study from jefferies found that  people are leaving state unemployment  roles more quickly in the states that  have already cut off the federal  expanded benefits  suggesting more are re-entering the  workforce but some economists say  the benefits are giving people time to  find the right job  and maybe even hold out for a better  offer when you look at the data  the states that have had the biggest  decreases in  unemployment are also states that have  very generous unemployment benefits so  there's not a direct correlation that  higher unemployment benefits are meaning  people aren't getting back to work  so today i'm pleased to announce an  incentive program that i feel is a  positive step  towards getting some people off  unemployment and back  into the workforce recently states have  started offering their own cash  incentives to residents rejoining the  workforce  colorado's program which had more than  eighteen thousand applicants paid up to  sixteen hundred dollars to people  returning to work full-time  still for many it's not about the money  concerns about the pandemic remain  specifically in face to face work and  child care is keeping millions of  parents  particularly women at home and frankly i  think people are kind of weary  this has been a really hard period of  time  that we've gone through and and you know  people are  sort of taking a moment to think about  what are my priorities  whatever the reason it's likely we'll  continue to see signing bonuses and  other incentives for as long as  businesses compete to fill these jobs  the increased wages economists say will  stick around even longer  if things are working we're going to  keep doing them but if they're not  solving our problem  we're going to have to come up with new  ways to to  skin this cat because we cannot  just post the jobs and assume people are  going to show up

